    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e0305118_8c805f3f",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 22,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T01:05:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Shouldn\u0027t the config be selected at board level i.e. if there is a mix of variants that support/don\u0027t support the AUX FW update, then only the variants which support it should enable the CONFIG_*.\n\nBTW, I was wondering, do you really need a function callback for this? Or can it be just a global array and a global count variable that can be accessed directly in host_command_get_aux_fw_chip_list? I see that used commonly in EC e.g. motion_sensors[] in board.c",
      "parentUuid": "07a6fe7d_0f32b99a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 18,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 22,
        "endChar": 1
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68e56ae1_7c61bff6",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 22,
      "author": {
        "id": 1302633
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T18:24:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Will define the chip info config in a global array so that any board that enables the CONFIG should define it.",
      "parentUuid": "e0305118_8c805f3f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 18,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 22,
        "endChar": 1
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "60ca9d91_2f23e93b",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T01:05:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do we want to print an error in case the number of slots that host has is less than what the EC wants to tell about? Or set a bit indicating more slots available to host? I know this isn\u0027t a problem right now, but just wondering if that should be accounted for somewhere in the form of error or information.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "02044507_81613c36",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T01:09:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Does this even need to be part of the API? Why not just send back as many records as you have... if that overflows the buffer on the other side, the host can detect that and decide how to deal with it.",
      "parentUuid": "60ca9d91_2f23e93b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "48960d91_afbaf05c",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T01:15:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do you mean copy all the records irrespective of the buffer size provided by the host?",
      "parentUuid": "02044507_81613c36",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2294ed2b_0e849f0d",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T01:26:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes. That should lead to a checksum error on the host side, I think (because it will only receive the top part of the packet)? And then it can still check num_aux_fw_chip in the response header and compare that to the buffer size it provided to find out that the result didn\u0027t fit.\n\nJust moves more error handling into the less constrained code base and saves a byte on the wire.",
      "parentUuid": "48960d91_afbaf05c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7f26250e_4951daf0",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1302633
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T18:24:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not sure where the space for the response buffer is allocated in EC. Won\u0027t copying irrespective of the buffer size lead to buffer overflow and memory corruption?",
      "parentUuid": "2294ed2b_0e849f0d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7fa2efb9_a057631b",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1302633
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T21:09:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Found that records up until args-\u003eresponse_max size can be copied over.\n\nAt the depthcharge side, all the 3 physical transports LPC, I2C, SPI check the response length and see if it can fit in the provided buffer. If the response length is too large, all of them return error to the caller of the ec_command. I hope the error won\u0027t stall the bus for future transactions.",
      "parentUuid": "7f26250e_4951daf0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3336b41a_bbfa9edc",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T21:55:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I mean, this is just normal transfer-protocol stuff, right? If anything the EC could do could cause a buffer overflow on the host side, we\u0027d have bigger problems than just figuring out how to prevent it for this command. I assume that is handled correctly (including error recovery) for all the transfer layers we support.",
      "parentUuid": "7fa2efb9_a057631b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4ff7193e_c59ca746",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T22:29:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we would result in a buffer overflow on the host, it would mostly be an overflow on the EC side but that can be avoided by checking against response_max.",
      "parentUuid": "3336b41a_bbfa9edc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dc143ab7_61921051",
        "filename": "common/aux_fw_chip_info.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-07T22:55:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I mean, you already have to account for that anyway, that\u0027s an orthogonal problem. If the host passes p-\u003enum_aux_fw_chip \u003d\u003d MAX_INT, and the EC somehow has a million TCPCs it wants to inform the host about, it still needs to check that they fit into its own buffer space. So yeah, should probably add a check for that, but it\u0027s a separate issue.",
      "parentUuid": "4ff7193e_c59ca746",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 37,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "d6229a233966429769f6867f4bd1ebc03518a563",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
